---
<Base
  title="Human-Directed Software Engineering"
  description="Human-Directed Software Engineering (HDSE) is an evolving methodology for directing AI-assisted implementation while preserving human accountability, verification, and durable engineering knowledge."
>
  <section class="wrap pagehero">
    <div class="eyebrow">An evolving methodology</div>
    <h1>Human-Directed Software Engineering</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Engineering software when implementation is increasingly automated.</p>
    <p class="lede">AI systems can now perform substantial portions of software implementation. That changes the engineer’s work, but it does not remove the need for engineering judgment. Human-Directed Software Engineering organizes development around explicit decisions, durable project knowledge, independent verification, and human accountability.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:0">HDSE is a working methodology I'm developing through software engineering practice. It draws on established engineering disciplines and is intended to evolve through practical use, published examples, and feedback. The primary audience is practicing engineers; technical leaders, instructors, students, and teams adopting AI tools are secondary audiences.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Code Generation Is Not Software Engineering</h2>
      <p>Writing code and engineering a software system overlap, but they are not the same job. Engineering includes deciding what to build, under which constraints, with which risks accepted, and with what evidence the result is fit for use.</p>
      <p>Requirements are often ambiguous. Correct-looking code can hide wrong assumptions about data, concurrency, or permissions. A passing test suite can miss essential behavior if the tests share the same misunderstandings as the code. Architectural shortcuts accumulate. Security and operational risk extend past individual functions into deployment, monitoring, and recovery.</p>
      <p>AI tools add their own complications: different outputs across runs, design decisions trapped in chat transcripts, and productivity claims that count only generation time while ignoring verification, integration, rework, and maintenance.</p>
      <p>Generated code is often useful. The harder question is how to keep judgment and evidence intact when implementation gets cheaper. HDSE is one practical answer: keep humans directing the work, keep important decisions in the repository, and verify results against evidence outside the model’s own assurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Working framework</div>
        <h2>Principles</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="principles">
      {principles.map((p) => (
        <article class="principle">
          <span class="number">{p.num}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{p.title}</h3>
            <p>{p.body}</p>
            <p class="practice"><strong>In practice.</strong> {p.practice}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>A Practical Workflow</h2>
      <p>HDSE uses an iterative workflow. Teams return to earlier stages when verification exposes a misunderstanding or when integration reveals a constraint that was not visible at the start. Verification belongs throughout the change, not only at the end.</p>
      <p>A one-line rename may compress several stages into a few careful minutes. A change that can corrupt records, cross a security boundary, or break an external contract should make each stage explicit enough that another engineer can reconstruct what was decided and why.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="workflow" aria-label="HDSE workflow stages">
      {workflow.map((s) => (
        <li class="workflowstep">
          <span class="number">{s.num}</span>
          <div>
            <strong>{s.title}</strong>
            <p>{s.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>What HDSE Looks Like in Practice</h2>
      <p>Here is an illustrative scenario for a subscription-management style application—meant to make the methodology concrete, not to report a measured case study.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="example">
      <h3>Initial request</h3>
      <p>“Add CSV import for subscriptions.” As stated, that request is under-specified. An assistant can still produce something that appears to work. HDSE treats that under-specification as a problem to solve before trusting the result.</p>

      <h3>Define and specify</h3>
      <p>Before implementation, the accountable engineer clarifies the outcomes that matter:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Accepted CSV schema and column meanings</li>
        <li>Required and optional fields</li>
        <li>Duplicate handling rules</li>
        <li>Behavior for invalid rows</li>
        <li>Import size limits</li>
        <li>Transaction and rollback behavior</li>
        <li>User-facing error reporting</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Those decisions can live in an issue, a short specification note, or acceptance criteria attached to the change. The form matters less than the durability and inspectability of the record.</p>

      <h3>Delegate and implement</h3>
      <p>An AI coding agent may implement the parser, validation logic, and application integration within those documented constraints. The human still owns the boundaries of the task, the choice of what to delegate, and the decision to accept the result. Areas that affect billing integrity, permissions, or irreversible data writes deserve closer human attention during implementation, not only at the end.</p>

      <h3>Verify and integrate</h3>
      <p>Tests cover valid rows, malformed files, duplicate records, partial failures, and data integrity. Additional checks may include inspecting SQL effects, confirming error messages are actionable, trying imports that intentionally violate limits, and confirming the feature behaves correctly alongside existing create/update flows. Integration asks whether the import respects current authorization, logging, and operational limits—not only whether the parser unit tests pass.</p>

      <h3>Review and preserve</h3>
      <p>The human evaluates import semantics, failure behavior, performance implications for large files, and whether the change fits the surrounding architecture. Release remains a human decision. The repository ends with the implementation, tests, usage documentation, and any significant design decisions. A later engineer—or a later agent—should not need the original chat to understand why the import behaves as it does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Division of labor</div>
        <h2>What Changes When AI Writes More Code?</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="compare" role="group" aria-label="Automatable tasks versus accountable engineering responsibilities">
      <div class="comparecol">
        <h3>Increasingly automatable</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Boilerplate implementation</li>
          <li>Routine code transformations</li>
          <li>Initial test generation</li>
          <li>Documentation drafts</li>
          <li>Exploring alternatives</li>
          <li>Repetitive refactoring</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="comparecol">
        <h3>Still requires accountable engineering</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Defining the actual problem</li>
          <li>Choosing architectural boundaries</li>
          <li>Evaluating test adequacy</li>
          <li>Confirming accuracy and completeness</li>
          <li>Selecting appropriate tradeoffs</li>
          <li>Managing system-wide risk</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <p>AI can assist with items in both columns. The difference is which work can be delegated for execution and which responsibilities still need an accountable human.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Contexts</div>
        <h2>Applying HDSE</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="audience">
      <article>
        <h3>Practicing Engineers</h3>
        <p>A practical development discipline for using AI assistance without losing codebase understanding, reviewability, or control. The day-to-day payoff is fewer “mystery merges” and clearer evidence that a change does what was intended.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Teams and Organizations</h3>
        <p>Shared conventions for repository context, task definition, review, verification, operational risk, and accountability. Organizations adopting coding agents need norms that survive staff changes and tool changes—not only individual prompt skill.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Educators and Students</h3>
        <p>A way to teach software engineering when generating code is no longer a reliable proxy for understanding. Education is one application of HDSE; the primary audience remains practicing engineers.</p>
        <a class="textlink" href="/teaching">Teaching interests</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Learning and Applying HDSE</h2>
      <p>Three learning paths are in development—planned sequences of practice and materials aimed at transferable engineering judgment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="paths">
      {paths.map((p) => (
        <div class="pathrow">
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Labs and Reference Work</h2>
      <p>HDSE should be demonstrated in real repositories. Planned labs will cover requirements and specifications, domain modeling, architectural decisions, agent-directed implementation, testing and verification, and repository-first documentation—material another engineer can clone, inspect, and critique.</p>
      <p>The first planned reference project is Writer IDE, a collaborative writing application focused on structured content, shared universes, and canonical project knowledge. Links will be added when a public repository is available to inspect.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Open Questions</h2>
      <p>HDSE continues to be refined through use. Open questions include how much specification different task sizes need; which verification approaches catch agent-generated defects well; how teams measure productivity after review and rework; how engineers keep deep understanding when implementation is delegated; how the methodology should change as models improve; and which practices remain useful even without AI.</p>
      <p>Answers may differ for solo developers, product teams, regulated environments, and classrooms.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Related sources</div>
        <h2>Further Reading</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <p>Related research and documentation on curricula, AI-assisted delivery, repository context, productivity measurement, and AI risk. Useful context for the same problem space; not endorsements of HDSE.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="readinglist">
      {reading.map((r) => (
        <div>
          <a class="textlink" href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{r.title}</a>
          <p>{r.note}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="calloutlink"><a class="textlink" href="/teaching">Related teaching interests</a> · <a class="textlink" href="/work">Selected work</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
